            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house is pretty air tight, however there are some nuisance areas where air leakage was observed. Air leakage was observed around the flue of your pellet stove (where it goes through the wall). High temperature silicone can used to seal the flue/wall from the interior. Be sure to use a high temperature silicon, as standard silicone may not hold up well to the exhaust temperatures of the pellet stove, and could pose a potential fire hazard. 



Air leakage was also observed through the master bedroom ensuite exhaust fans. I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STARÂ® rated model.



Some air leakage was observed along the floor header of the basement. It appeared some of the poly has pulled away from the header. I recommend to reapply acoustical caulking between the header and poly, and use construction tape to hold the poly in place. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 40&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;1.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;











&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation, as well as 2x2 strapping with no insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R15 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 520&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;17.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;













&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <foundation>

Your basement pony walls appear to be 2x6 framing with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. Along with the above wall insulation, I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 3 inches (R15)of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation to the pony walls, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 170&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;5.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 625 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total foundation wall area including crawlspaces (when applicable), and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</foundation>
                <HotWaterSystem>

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Current Oil Boiler and Domestic Hot Water&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



You currently have a 182 litre electric hot water tank, which is also indirectly heated by the Oil Boiler. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly.

It would be ideal to use your boiler to heat your hot water year round, instead of turning off the boiler during the summer months and using the electricity to heat your hot water (winter is assumed to be 9 months of the year).



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By using your current boiler to heat your hot water year round, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 250&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;95% Propane Boiler and Domestic Hot Water&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



If you choose to upgrade your boiler to a condensing 95% AFUE Propane boiler, and continue to heat your hot water indrectly with the new boiler, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 190&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;6.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. 























&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil fired boiler was originally rated at 86.1% Steady State. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether, as well as the combustion air supply pipe.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 740&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;19.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Pellet Stove&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



&lt;b&gt;With the Current Oil Boiler&lt;/b&gt;

By using your pellet stove for 50% of your heating load, you could potentially save $ 690 per year in energy savings.



&lt;b&gt;With a 95% Condensing Propane Boiler&lt;/b&gt;

By using your pellet stove for 50% of your heating load, you could potentially save $ 500 per year in energy savings.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.12 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord 

Pellets - $5.67 per bag



